From 394e3ae5ab2b8006666b2f12327e165de035287c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 21:56:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 37/56] vulkan/wsi: the Horizon (nwindow) backend
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 items 1-10 (the Horizon WSI)
Why: nothing in Mesa presents through Horizon's VI compositor. This adds
 the backend for VK_ICD_WSI_PLATFORM_VI: surface, capabilities, formats,
 present modes, and a swapchain with explicit slot ownership, two
 present paths and a decision between them that is reported rather than
 assumed.
 Four things in it are answers to defects
 Four things in it are answers to defects the reference ports share,
 and each is written where the defect was:
  - the surface owns the window's scanout registration, so two
    swapchains over one window hand it over rather than colliding. The
    reference solved the same problem with a file-scope pointer;
  - release_images cancels dequeued slots back to the compositor instead
    of clearing a busy flag, which leaks them from the BufferQueue's
    point of view;
  - the queue result is checked, so a failed present is not reported as
    VK_SUCCESS;
  - there is no fall-through: every present either queues, copies, or
    returns an error.
 It drives libnx's IGraphicBufferProducer wrappers rather than NWindow's
 dequeue/queue, because NWindow keeps one `cur_slot` and Vulkan makes
 two acquired images valid usage on a three-image swapchain with
 minImageCount = 2. Registration and teardown still go through
 nwindowConfigureBuffer and nwindowReleaseBuffers.
 wsi_device gains a `horizon` sub-struct of driver callbacks, in the
 same shape as the existing win32 and metal ones, and
 WSI_IMAGE_TYPE_HORIZON — a renderable image the window system scans out
 as it stands, with no modifier, no dma-buf and no CPU mapping.
 The dequeue policy is one policy at every timeout: bqDequeueBuffer is
 always asked async (as libnx does), a would-block answer covers the
 wider retryable result set (WouldBlock, NoInit, InvalidOperation), and
 the loop idles with svcSleepThread(refresh/8) between attempts until
 the caller's deadline — never a blocking IPC that a concurrent
 CancelBuffer cannot interrupt, and never a permanently-signalled event
 wait that spins. Teardown closes the framebuffer only from the
 window's owner, under the lock, in release_window — closing it from a
 swapchain that lost the window mid-recreation tore down the survivor's
 registration. The registered block-linear layout is logged when it is
 decided, against the VkDevice (the swapchain does not exist yet), and
 the copy fallback says when it cannot deliver the present mode it was
 asked for.
Evidence: cross build of build/mesa-nvk with -Dplatforms=vi
 (aarch64-none-elf-gcc 15.2.0 from ghcr.io/d3fau4/nx-dev, meson 1.9.1);
 wsi_horizon.c.o is in libvulkan_wsi.a and wsi_CreateViSurfaceNN is
 defined in it. The geometry constants are libnx's framebufferCreate,
 which is the known-working producer on this platform, and the
 num_ints field is the one the reference confirmed on hardware: left at
 zero, bqSetPreallocatedBuffer succeeds and the first dequeue never
 returns.
 ON CONSOLE, accumulated across the runs of 2026-08-05 to 2026-08-11:
 runs 11-13 present 90 of 90 frames at a mean of 16664 us with 89 of 89
 intervals inside 10% of a 60 Hz refresh, zero-copy, through
 vkCreateViSurfaceNN over the default window; run 16 passes
 t_vk_swapchain 125/125 and t_nwindow 119/119 in one build; run 17
 drives one swapchain from two threads for 600 frames (t_vk_wsi_mt
 50/50); run 21 passes t_vk_suboptimal 273/273 with 0073's 188
 client-invisible warnings taken to zero; the blocking-dequeue
 experiment that motivated the retry loop is
 experiment that motivated the retry loop is on record (a dequeue that
 never returned).
Upstream: no — it is a window system that exists on one platform.

Absorbs (2026-08-23 series compaction; full history — including the
dequeue-policy round trip this paragraph summarises — in
mesa-patches/'s own git log):

- 0055 "review corrections and a way to force the copy path"
  (MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_FORCE_COPY; its nvk_wsi.c hunk lives with the
  VK_NN_vi_surface patch).
- 0057 "a swapchain image's size is checked before it narrows"
  (VkDeviceSize into uint32_t in create_image_mem).
- 0059/0061/0062/0063/0064/0067 — the dequeue saga, whose surviving
  state is the policy described above: 0059 widened the retryable
  result set (hardware 2026-08-05); 0061-0063 introduced, sliced and
  re-introduced a blocking second mode; 0064 replaced the
  permanently-signalled eventWait with the refresh/8 sleep (hardware
  2026-08-05); 0067 deleted the second mode entirely — run 13
  (2026-08-08) presented 90 of 90 on two images with async-only
  dequeue, which retired it.
- 0065 "four findings from the PR 8 review" (NATIVE_WINDOW_IN_USE;
  IMMEDIATE takes a third image; a failed cancel is not FREE).
- 0066 "the eleven driver findings from the branch review" (the
  `presentable` data race, the lock gap in destroy, stride-covers-width,
  IMMEDIATE on the copy path, out-of-range slot).
- 0070 "only the window's owner may close the framebuffer" (hardware,
  run 17: the defect t_vk_wsi_mt found).
- 0071 "report the layout that was registered" and 0073 "the swapchain
  decision is logged against the device" (run 21 took the 188
  client-invisible warnings to zero).
- 0072 "closing the framebuffer is releasing the window" (the third,
  unlocked framebufferClose caller, PR 9 review).
- 0075 "say when IMMEDIATE is a mode the copy fallback cannot deliver"
  (run 25: 8264 us against FIFO's 16577 us over 240 frames each).
